`gen --fault` is double-guarded (#163), and both halves refuse **before** a
session is opened — which is what lets them be offline cases.

An unknown fault kind is refused by name, the vocabulary spelled out (spray's
decline precedent, applied to a flag). It exits **2** since #307: a kind
outside a closed vocabulary is this tool refusing your input, and on `gen` a 1
means "some samples were refused on the wire", which is a fact about a run
that happened.

```console
$ zenctl gen --fault scramble
? 2
Error: --fault "scramble": is not a known fault kind — known kinds: truncate, wrong-type, extra-field, unregistered-key, wrong-qos, missing-encoding, unstamped

```

The first guard: fault injection is deliberately non-conforming traffic, so it
may only run knowingly. No `--i-know`, no faults.

```console
$ zenctl gen --fault truncate
? 2
Error: --fault injects deliberately non-conforming traffic — that is consumer-robustness testing on a bus you own, not a default. Pass --i-know to mean it.

```

The second guard: the target must be **typed on this command line**. This
corpus exports `ZENCTL_BASE=` — a bus the shell was pointed at, exactly the
ambient default the guard exists to refuse — and clap folds that env var into
`--base` before the derive struct exists, so the guard asks the `ArgMatches`
where the value came from (`ValueSource::CommandLine`, the
`refuse_foreign_format` seam) rather than whether one exists. Before that fix,
this invocation sailed past the guard on the strength of an exported variable.

```console
$ zenctl gen --fault truncate --i-know
? 2
Error: --fault refuses the ambient target: type the bus on this command line with --base or an endpoint (--connect/--listen/--zenoh-config). An exported ZENCTL_BASE or a named context is whatever bus the shell was pointed at, and faults must never land there by default.

```

## One `--i-know` per verb, and `gen` had two guards on one flag

There is a second, unrelated guard here: a run wider than ten subjects is a
fleet-wide impersonation. Until #307 the same `--i-know` discharged both — so
somebody widening a perfectly benign run past the threshold typed `--i-know`,
and from that moment `--fault` was armed on the same command line. An
acknowledgement flag must acknowledge exactly one thing. `--wide` carries the
width guard now, and the graver guard kept the name. That half needs a plan,
and a plan needs a registry, so it is not an offline case — `gen --help` in
`help-wire.trycmd` is where both flags are pinned.
